      <h3 class="section-title">Reminders</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Future developments. For <span data-field="publication_number">{{ publication_number }}</span>, such as legislation enacted after it was published, go to <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Pub524</a>.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Photographs of missing children. The Internal Revenue Service is a proud partner with the <a href="#" style="color: blue;">National Center for Missing & Exploited Children (NCMEC)</a>. Photographs of missing children selected by the Center may appear in this publication on pages that would otherwise be blank. You can help bring these children home by looking at the photographs and calling 800-THE-LOST (800-843-5678) if you recognize a child.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Introduction</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you qualify, you may be able to reduce the tax you owe by taking the credit for the elderly or the disabled. This publication explains:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 12pt; list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Who qualifies for the credit for the elderly or the disabled.</li>
        <li>How to figure the credit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You are age 65 or older at the end of <span data-field="tax_year">{{ tax_year }}</span>, or</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You are retired on permanent and total disability and have taxable disability income.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Comments and suggestions.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">We welcome your comments about this publication and suggestions for future editions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Getting answers to your tax questions.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you have a tax question not answered by this publication or the How to Get Tax Help section at the end of this publication, go to <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Help/ITA</a> where you can find topics by using the search feature or viewing the categories listed.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Getting tax forms, instructions, and publications.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Go to <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Forms</a> to download current and prior-year forms, instructions, and publications.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Ordering tax forms, instructions, and publications.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Go to <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/OrderForms</a> to order current forms, instructions, and publications. You can also call 800-822-1722 to order your forms and publications as soon as possible. Don't resubmit requests you've already sent us. You can get forms and publications faster online.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Useful items</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You may want to see:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 12pt; list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="link_pub554">{{ link_pub554 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="link_form1040sr">{{ link_form1040sr }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="link_schedule1">{{ link_schedule1 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">See <a href="#" style="color: blue;">How to Get Tax Help</a>, near the end of this publication, for information about getting this publication and these forms.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 55%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Qualified Individual</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You are a qualified individual for this credit if you are a U.S. citizen or resident alien, and either of the following applies:</p>
      <ol style="font-size: 12pt; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>You were age 65 or older at the end of <span data-field="tax_year">{{ tax_year }}</span> and all three of the following statements are true:
          <ul style="font-size: 11pt; list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You are retired on permanent and total disability (ex-plained later).</li>
            <li>You've received taxable disability income for <span data-field="tax_year">{{ tax_year }}</span>.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>You are age 65 or older at the end of <span data-field="tax_year">{{ tax_year }}</span>, or</li>
        <li>You are retired on permanent and total disability.</li>
      </ol>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You can use <span data-field="figure_a">{{ figure_a }}</span> and <span data-field="table_1">{{ table_1 }}</span> as guides to see if you are eligible for the credit. Use <span data-field="Figure_A_link">{{ Figure_A_link }}</span> to see if you are a qualified individual. If you are, go to <span data-field="Table_1_link">{{ Table_1_link }}</span> to make sure your income isn't too high to take the credit.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Income Limits</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You can take the credit only if you file Form 1040 or Form 1040-SR. You can't take the credit if you file Form 1040-NR.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">For more information, see <span data-field="link_income_limits">{{ link_income_limits }}</span>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Are You Eligible for the Credit?</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You can take the credit for the elderly or the disabled if you meet both of the following requirements.</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 12pt; list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>You are a qualified individual.</li>
        <li>Your income isn't more than certain limits.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="section-title">Figure A. Are You a Qualified Individual?</h3>
      <div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
        <img src="placeholder_flowchart.png" alt="Flowchart" style="max-width: 100%;">
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">*However, you may be able to claim this credit even if you don't meet all the requirements listed in Figure A, if you meet the requirements listed in the following sections.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">*If you were a nonresident alien at any time during the tax year and were married to a U.S. citizen or resident alien at the end of the tax year, you may be able to claim this credit if you meet the requirements listed in the following sections.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 55%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Married Persons</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Generally, if you are married at the end of the tax year, you and your spouse must file a joint return to take the credit. However, if you and your spouse lived apart all times during the tax year, you may be able to take the credit.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Step 1. Determine Initial Amount</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">To figure the credit, you must first determine your initial amount using lines 10 through 12. Your initial amount is the amount you would receive if you were eligible for the credit.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you are single, head of household, or married filing separately, your initial amount is determined using <span data-field="Table_2_link">{{ Table_2_link }}</span> for your filing status or your taxable disability income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you are married filing jointly, your initial amount is determined using <span data-field="Table_2_link">{{ Table_2_link }}</span> for your filing status.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You can take the credit only if you file Form 1040 or Form 1040-SR. You can't take the credit if you file Form 1040-NR.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Step 2. Total Certain Nontaxable Pensions and Benefits</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Step 2 is to figure the total amount of nontaxable social security and certain other nontaxable pensions, annuities, and disability benefits you received during the year. You must reduce the initial amount you determined in Step 1 by these payments.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Enter these nontaxable payments on lines 13a or 13b and total them on line 13c. If you are married filing jointly, both you and your spouse must report these payments.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Worksheets are provided in the Instructions for Form 1040-SR to help you determine if any of your so-cial security benefits are taxable.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Include the following nontaxable payments in the amounts you enter on line 13a or 13b:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 12pt; list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Nontaxable social security payments. This is the non-taxable portion of your social security benefits shown in box 5 of Form SSA-1099.</li>
        <li>Certain nontaxable pensions, annuities, or disability benefits that are paid under a law administered by the VA. Don't include amounts received as a pension, an-nuitiy, or similar allowance for personal injuries or sick-ness resulting from active service in the armed forces.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Step 3. Determine Excess Adjusted Gross Income</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You must reduce the initial amount you determined in Step 1 by your excess adjusted gross income. Figure your excess adjusted gross income on lines 14 through 17.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Subtract from your adjusted gross income (Form 1040 or 1040-SR, line 11) the following amount shown for your filing status:</p>
      <table style="width: 100%;">
        <tr>
          <th style="width: 50%;">If you are single, head of household, or married filing separately:</th>
          <td style="width: 50%; text-align: right;">$7,500 if you are single, head of household, or married filing separately and you lived apart all times during the tax year.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>If you are married filing jointly:</th>
          <td style="text-align: right;">$15,000 if you are married filing jointly and you lived apart all times during the tax year.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Divide the result of (1) by 2.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Step 4. Determine the Total of Steps 2 and 3</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">To determine if you can take the credit, you must add (on line 18) the amounts you figured in Step 2 (line 13c) and Step 3 (line 17).</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Step 5. Determine Your Credit</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Subtract the amount determined in Step 4 (line 18) from the initial amount determined in Step 1 (line 12), and multiply the result by 15% (0.15).</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">In certain cases, the amount of your credit may be limited. See <span data-field="Limit_link">{{ Limit_link }}</span>.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">Example. You are 65 years old and your spouse is 64. Your spouse isn't disabled. You file a joint return on Form 1040. Your adjusted gross income is $14,630. Together you received $3,200 from social security, which was nontaxable. You figure the credit as follows:</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">(Calculation details omitted for brevity in this template).</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 55%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">Credit Figured for You</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You can figure the credit yourself or the IRS will figure it for you. If you want the IRS to figure it for you, fill out the instructions and the instructions in <span data-field="Credit_Figured_Link">{{ Credit_Figured_Link }}</span>.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you can take the credit and you want the IRS to figure it for you, fill out the instructions and the instructions in <span data-field="Credit_Figured_Link">{{ Credit_Figured_Link }}</span>.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you are filing jointly, you must use the worksheet in Part I of Schedule R to apply the credit. If you are filing separately, you must use the worksheet in Part II of Schedule R to apply the credit.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">There are five steps in Part II to determine the amount of your credit.</p>
      <ol style="font-size: 12pt; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Determine your initial amount (lines 10-12).</li>
        <li>Determine the total of nontaxable social security and certain other nontaxable pensions, annuities, and disability benefits you received (lines 13a, 13b, and 13c).</li>
        <li>Determine your excess adjusted gross income (lines 14-17).</li>
        <li>Determine the total of Steps 2 and 3 (line 18).</li>
        <li>Determine your credit.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 class="section-title">Examples</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">The following example illustrates the credit for the elderly or the disabled. The initial amounts are taken from <span data-field="Table_2_link">{{ Table_2_link }}</span>.</p>
      <table style="width: 100%;">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">Example 1.</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">Amount</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>1. Initial amount based on filing status</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$5,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2. Taxable disability pension</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$14,200</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>3. Nontaxable social security</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$5,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4. Excess adjusted gross income</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$3,400</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>5. Total of Steps 2 and 3</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$4,100</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>6. Subtract line 5 from line 3</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$900</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>7. Multiply line 6 by 15% (0.15)</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$135</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>8. Enter the amount from the Credit Worksheet—Line 21 in the Instructions for Schedule R</td>
            <td style="text-align: right;">$46</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h3 class="section-title">U.S. Citizen or Resident Alien</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You must be a U.S. citizen or resident alien (or be treated as a resident alien) to take the credit. Generally, you can take the credit if you were a nonresident alien at any time during the tax year.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Exceptions.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">You may be able to take the credit if you are a nonresident alien who is married to a U.S. citizen or resi-dent alien at the end of the tax year and you and your spouse choose to treat you as a U.S. resident alien. If you make that choice, both you and your spouse are taxed on your worldwide income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">For information on these choices, see chapter 1 of Pub. 519.</p>
      <h3 class="section-title">Disability Income.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">If you are under age 65, you must also have taxable disability income to qualify for the credit.</p>
    </div>
